<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/advanced/paleoclimate-and-ice-ages">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Paleoclimatology recovers <strong>climate history from physical archives</strong> — ice,
    sediment, rings, rock. It discovered the ice ages' astronomical rhythm, revealed that
    climate can jump, and provides the out-of-sample tests that make climate projections
    trustworthy. The syllabus assigns this lesson two full units: glaciers as machines
    (14.1–14.2 — how ice forms, flows, erodes and deposits, and the landforms it leaves) and
    the ice-age story proper (14.3), which the global-climate-change unit then reuses as its
    evidence base (15.2 — the archives that prove past change — and 15.3 — the external and
    internal drivers). The advanced skill is reading the archives the way a professional
    does: knowing what each proxy actually measures, where it is precise, and where it is
    being asked a question it cannot fully answer.
  </p>
  <p>
    The connecting insight between the two halves: <strong>ice is both the machine and the
    archive</strong>. The same material properties that let glaciers carve continents (ice
    deforms under its own weight, flows downhill, scrapes and scours) also preserve annual
    layers and trapped air — and the glacial landforms of one section are the field
    evidence that dated the ice ages for the other. When Louis Agassiz proposed in 1837
    that Swiss erratics and U-shaped valleys were the work of vanished continental ice,
    geology's first great climate revolution began with landform reading.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the archives, the Milankovitch metronome, the jumps, the analogues" widget="equationStep" config={paleoCfg} />

  <h2>Glaciers: how ice machines work (14.1–14.2)</h2>
  <p>
    A glacier forms where <strong>accumulation exceeds ablation</strong> — snowfall packs
    to firn, then to ice, and the surplus survives summer melt. Above the equilibrium
    line the glacier gains; below it, loses; the ice itself flows from the former to the
    latter, deforming plastically under its own weight (a few metres to hundreds of
    metres per year, faster where meltwater lubricates the bed) and sliding when the
    bed is wet. Mass balance is the machine's fuel gauge: advance when positive, retreat
    when negative — which is why glacier fronts are the visual long-term thermometer the
    climate chapters keep citing (most of Earth's monitored glaciers are now losing
    mass). The erosion toolkit: <strong>abrasion</strong> (rock-flour sandpapering) and
    <strong>plucking</strong> (freeze-thaw prizing blocks loose on the lee side of
    obstacles) carve <strong>U-shaped valleys</strong> (V-shaped river valleys
    over-deepened), <strong>cirques</strong> (armchair basins at the accumulation zone),
    the knife-edge <strong>arêtes</strong> and pyramidal <strong>horns</strong> between
    them (the Matterhorn), and <strong>fjords</strong> (valleys scoured below sea level,
    then drowned). The deposition side reads like a sedimentologist's inventory:
    <strong>till</strong> (unsorted rubble dumped directly), <strong>moraines</strong>
    (terminal, lateral, medial — the glacier's tidelines), <strong>drumlins</strong>
    (whale-backed hills aligned with flow), <strong>erratics</strong> (boulders far from
    their source — Agassiz's smoking gun), <strong>eskers</strong> (subglacial-stream
    gravel ridges) and <strong>outwash plains</strong> (the sorted, stratified
    downstream fans). Every one of these is also a proxy: terminal moraine positions
    map past ice extent; erratics trace flow direction; and their worldwide consistency
    is what established the glacial epochs.
  </p>

  <h2>The archives: reading temperature from the past (15.2)</h2>
  <p>
    Each archive is an instrument with its own calibration, resolution and reach.
    <strong>Ice cores</strong> are the crown jewels: annual layers count like tree rings,
    the water's δ¹⁸O/Deuterium ratio is a thermometer (the isotope arithmetic in Going
    deeper), and — uniquely — the trapped bubbles are <em>samples of ancient air</em>,
    giving direct CO₂ and CH₄ measurements back 800,000 years (EPICA Dome C, Antarctica).
    <strong>Ocean sediment cores</strong> reach 100+ million years: the shells of benthic
    foraminifera carry δ¹⁸O that mixes temperature with global ice volume (the two
    signals must be disentangled — one of the craft's real subtleties), and their
    species assemblages are ecological thermometers. <strong>Tree rings</strong> give
    annual, absolutely dated width-and-density records for ~12,000 years;
    <strong>speleothems</strong> (cave stalagmites) and <strong>corals</strong> add
    regional rainfall and sea level; <strong>loess</strong> (windblown dust) records the
    ice ages' dry, windy continents. No single archive suffices — the power is in
    <strong>multi-proxy reconstruction</strong>, where archives that disagree force
    better understanding of both.
  </p>

  <h2>The astronomical metronome: Milankovitch cycles (14.3, 15.3)</h2>
  <p>
    The external driver is orbital. Earth's orbit changes shape (<strong>eccentricity</strong>,
    ~100,000-year rhythm), its tilt wobbles (<strong>obliquity</strong>, 41,000 years,
    between 22.1° and 24.5°), and its axis precesses like a spun top (<strong>~19–23,000
    years</strong>) — together redistributing <em>where and when</em> sunlight falls
    (total annual insolation barely changes). The theory (Milutin Milankovitch, computed
    by hand in the 1910s–40s) says ice ages are paced by summer insolation at 65°N:
    cool northern summers let winter snow survive, and ice builds. The confirmation is
    one of geology's classic results — Hays, Imbrie and Shackleton (1976) found the
    three orbital frequencies in 450,000 years of deep-sea isotope records, "the
    Pacemaker of the Ice Ages". But the orbit only turns the key; the amplitude is
    amplified by feedbacks the geochemical-cycles machinery supplies: CO₂ falls from
    ~280 to ~180 ppm as oceans and boreal lands absorb it in the cold (the greenhouse
    feedback that deepens the cold), ice–albedo brightens the planet, and dust
    fertilises iron-limited oceans. The honest caveat is famous: <strong>the 100,000-year
    problem</strong> — eccentricity's insolation effect is the weakest of the three,
    yet the last ~800,000 years of cycles follow its rhythm, and no fully accepted
    mechanism explains why (one of the field's standing open problems). The Last Glacial
    Maximum, 20,000 years ago, shows the whole machinery worked out:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the LGM worked example — from ice extent to radiative forcing to a sensitivity test of the whole climate system" widget="equationStep" config={lgmCfg} />

  <h2>Dating the archives: how the ages are pinned</h2>
  <p>
    A proxy without a calendar is a curiosity, so the discipline invests as much in
    chronology as in thermometry. <strong>Layer counting</strong> is the gold standard
    where it applies: annual ice layers (visible as chemistry and isotope cycles) and
    tree rings date events to the year, and the D-O events' decadal resolution exists
    precisely because Greenland ice can be counted. <strong>Radiocarbon</strong> dates
    the last ~50,000 years (organic material; calibrated against tree rings, since the
    production rate wobbles); <strong>uranium–thorium</strong> dates cave calcite and
    corals to ~500,000 years (U decays in, Th stays put — clean arithmetic);
    <strong>potassium–argon and argon–argon</strong> date volcanic layers, which
    time-bracket sediment records; <strong>magnetostratigraphy</strong> (the flips of
    Earth's magnetic field, dated independently) provides global tie-points; and
    <strong>orbital tuning</strong> — aligning a record's cycles with the known
    Milankovitch clock — extends the framework to millions of years. The craft is in
    the cross-checks: archives dated several ways must agree within their error bars,
    and where they do not (the radiocarbon plateau, ice-flow-thinning corrections at
    depth), the arguments are the published papers.
  </p>

  <h2>Climate that jumps: abrupt change</h2>
  <p>
    The ice cores' second revolution was speed. Greenland cores record
    <strong>Dansgaard–Oeschger events</strong>: warmings of 8–16 °C (annual mean,
    Greenland) within decades, ~25 of them through the last glacial period — the
    climate flipping between modes, most plausibly via reorganisations of Atlantic
    circulation (AMOC) and sea-ice cover. Heinrich events (armadas of ice-rafted
    debris from surging Laurentide ice) and the <strong>Younger Dryas</strong>
    (~12,900–11,700 years ago: a 1,200-year return to near-glacial cold as the
    post-glacial meltwater apparently throttled the Atlantic circulation) are the
    same lesson at different scales. The implications cut two ways: the Holocene's
    remarkable stability is not a law of nature but a fortunate regime; and the
    ocean-circulation levers that once flipped climate are the same ones climate
    models watch today under fresh-water loading from Greenland melt — the AMOC
    question of the climate-modelling and ocean chapters.
  </p>

  <h2>Deep time: the analogue library</h2>
  <p>
    Further back, the archives span every experiment Earth has run. <strong>Snowball
    Earth</strong> episodes (~720–635 and ~2,200 million years ago) put ice at the
    tropics; the escape mechanism is the weathering-thermostat pushed to its extreme —
    volcanic CO₂ accumulating for millions of years under ice that no longer weathers
    it down — an end-to-end demonstration of the greenhouse physics and the carbon
    cycle's long arm. The <strong>Paleocene–Eocene Thermal Maximum</strong> (~56
    million years ago) released 1,000–3,000 gigatonnes of carbon over a few thousand
    years, warming the deep ocean 5–8 °C, acidifying it (benthic forams suffered a mass
    extinction on the seafloor) and raising sea level; the carbon-isotope excursion
    fingerprints a light-carbon source (methane hydrates and/or volcanism-fed). It is
    the closest natural analogue to our emissions — with the sobering arithmetic that
    we are injecting carbon <em>roughly ten times faster</em> than the PETM did. The
    <strong>Holocene</strong> (11,700 years of unusual stability) is the only climate
    agriculture has ever practised — civilisation as a fair-weather construction —
    and its tiny wiggles (the Medieval Warm Period, the Little Ice Age) are the
    historical chapters' texture. Sea level is the integrator that remembers: −120 m
    at the LGM (Beringia walkable; the anthropology lesson's migration routes are a
    paleoclimate map), +10s of metres during past warm periods. The archives
    collectively answer the question the climate-modelling chapter needs answered:
    <em>how sensitive is this system?</em> — and they grade its projections
    out-of-sample.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "climate has always changed, so today's change is
    natural." The archives are precisely what falsify that inference: they show
    <em>what natural change looks like</em> — paced by orbit, amplified slowly by
    CO₂ feedbacks, at rates of ~80 ppm CO₂ per thousand years — and today's +120 ppm
    in a single century, with an isotopic fossil-carbon signature, has no
    counterpart in 800,000 years of direct record. The past does not excuse the
    present; it is the control experiment that identifies the present's cause.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The isotope thermometer, derived.</strong> Evaporation preferentially lifts
    light water (H₂¹⁶O) — heavier H₂¹⁸O evaporates less readily and rains out sooner.
    Air masses therefore lose ¹⁸O as they travel poleward and cool (Rayleigh
    distillation), so the snow's δ¹⁸O at a site tracks the temperature it was
    transported through; the modern spatial calibration (≈0.7‰ per °C in Greenland)
    anchors the paleothermometer, with corrections for the ocean's own isotopic state
    and for glacial wind patterns. The same physics in <em>seawater</em> makes benthic
    δ¹⁸O an ice-volume gauge: when 10¹⁹ kg of isotopically light water sits in ice
    sheets, the ocean's δ¹⁸O rises — the mixed signal that makes deep-sea records
    both powerful and argumentative.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Thresholds, hysteresis and the ice-sheet question.</strong> Ice sheets do
    not scale linearly with temperature: they have <strong>hysteresis</strong> (a
    warming that melts them is not reversed by the equivalent cooling, because
    accumulation zones, albedo and bed elevation all shift), which is why sea-level
    commitment is measured in metres per century of warming held — Greenland holds
    ~7 m of sea level, West Antarctica ~5 m, East Antarctica tens — and why the
    equilibrium response to today's warming (still unspent) is the deepest concern in
    the projections. Paleoclimate provides the calibration points: sea level was
    ~6–9 m higher in the last interglacial (~125,000 years ago, only ~1 °C warmer
    globally than pre-industrial) — a measured warning about where small warmings
    send big ice.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> The 100,000-year problem (why the strongest cycle
    follows the weakest forcing) remains unsolved after fifty years; the role of CO₂
    <em>causing</em> versus amplifying terminations is being narrowed by
    synchronised ice-core chronologies; the AMOC's stability under fresh-water forcing
    is debated across model generations (tipping-point estimates range from unlikely
    this century to likely at 2–4 °C); Antarctic ice-sheet dynamics (marine ice-sheet
    instability — where the bed slopes backward, retreat can self-accelerate) are the
    largest wildcard in sea-level projection; and the search for older ice (million-
    year cores, currently being drilled) pushes the direct atmospheric record back
    through the mid-Pleistocene transition — the interval where the ice ages
    lengthened and the 100-kyr problem begins.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Hays, Imbrie &amp; Shackleton, <em>Science</em> (1976); EPICA ice cores, <em>Nature</em> (2004–2008); Zachos et al. on the PETM (2001); Tierney et al. on LGM sensitivity (2020); IPCC AR6 paleoclimate chapter.</p>
